# Comment Style

Multiple successive lines of `#` comments make code unreadable. Collapse them into a single line (or remove them entirely).

## Rules

- Never write two or more consecutive lines that start with `#` (ignoring leading whitespace).
- If a comment needs more content, keep it on ONE line — even if that line is long.
- Prefer no comment over a redundant/narrating one. Comments should only explain non-obvious intent, trade-offs, or constraints.
- The shebang (`#!/usr/bin/env ...`) and the immediately following module docstring/encoding line are exempt.
- This applies to inline `#` comments in Python, shell, YAML, etc. Docstrings (`"""..."""`) are unaffected.

## Examples

```python
# ❌ BAD — stacked comment lines
# Compute the shear-averaged spectrum by taking the FFT along azimuth,
# multiplying by the window, and inverse-transforming. This block also
# handles the wrap-around and normalizes by the coherent sum.
S = np.fft.ifft(np.fft.fft(x, axis=0) * w, axis=0)
```

```python
# ✅ GOOD — single line
# Shear-averaged spectrum: FFT(az) * window, then IFFT, normalized by coherent sum.
S = np.fft.ifft(np.fft.fft(x, axis=0) * w, axis=0)
```

```python
# ✅ GOOD — no comment when the code is self-explanatory
S = np.fft.ifft(np.fft.fft(x, axis=0) * w, axis=0)
```

## When updating existing code

- Merge adjacent comment lines into one line, keeping only the essential meaning.
- Delete narration comments that only restate what the next line of code does.
- Keep comments that explain *why*, not *what*.
